            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Conclusion First: Tiering is Not Optional, It's Essential</h2>
<p>Many teams start feeling overwhelmed when their account count exceeds 5: slow creative updates, chaotic top-up timing, abnormal accounts affecting the whole operation, time-consuming reconciliations... These problems aren't caused by the number of accounts, but by the lack of systematic tiering logic.</p>
<p><strong>The core value of account tiering is: maximizing ROI from limited operational resources while keeping risks within acceptable bounds.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>4 Key Dimensions for Account Tiering</h2>
<h3>1. Tier by Asset Scale: Determining Resource Investment Ratio</h3>
<p>Account spend and balance directly determine management priority.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Head Accounts</strong> (60%+ of team spend): Dedicated optimizers, highest creative update frequency, top-up priority guaranteed, daily financial reconciliation.</li>
<li><strong>Middle Accounts</strong> (20%-30% of spend): One optimizer manages 2-3 accounts, weekly creative updates, top-up warning 3 days in advance.</li>
<li><strong>Tail Accounts</strong> (under 10% of spend): Batch or minimal maintenance, focus on whether continued investment is worthwhile.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Operational Experience: A cross-border e-commerce team tiered their 10 accounts by spend. The top 3 head accounts contributed 78% of GMV. Middle accounts mainly tested new creatives and categories, while tail accounts were gradually streamlined to 3.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>2. Tier by Historical Performance: Determining Continued Investment</h3>
<p>Account historical ROAS and stability determine its lifecycle.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High-Performing Accounts</strong>: Long-term stable profitability. Core task is maximizing budget utilization with sufficient top-up to avoid downtime.</li>
<li><strong>Volatile Accounts</strong>: Need continuous monitoring with clear stop-loss thresholds (e.g., ROAS below 1.5 for 3 consecutive days).</li>
<li><strong>Low-Performing Accounts</strong>: Promptly analyze reasons—creative, landing page, or audience issues. Pause and reallocate budget if no improvement.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Tier by Risk Level: Determining Control Intensity</h3>
<p>Different accounts have varying ban risks, top-up risks, and financial risks.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High-Risk Accounts</strong>: New accounts, recent violation records, sensitive audience categories. Require separate monitoring group, lower top-up amounts, increased reconciliation frequency.</li>
<li><strong>Normal-Risk Accounts</strong>: Managed by standard procedures.</li>
<li><strong>Low-Risk Accounts</strong>: Long-term stable operation, good credit history. Can simplify approval processes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Tier by Team Role: Determining Operation Permissions</h3>
<p>Not every team member needs access to all accounts.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Admin</strong>: Full access and operation rights for all accounts—core permission.</li>
<li><strong>Lead Operator</strong>: Daily operations of head accounts with top-up rights.</li>
<li><strong>Assistant</strong>: Only operates middle and lower-tier accounts, no top-up rights, read-only reconciliation access.</li>
<li><strong>Finance</strong>: Reconciliation and top-up approval only, no operation access.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Permission tiering prevents operational errors and enables quick tracing of responsible parties when account issues occur.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Practical Tips: 3 Steps to Build a Tiering System</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1: Inventory Current Accounts</strong><br />
Use Excel or management tools to list all account basics: spend scale, historical ROAS, current balance, account age, operations lead.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Define Tiering Criteria</strong><br />
Create a cross-matrix using asset scale and historical performance as two dimensions. Divide into 4-6 tiers, each with clear management rules.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Implement Supporting Tools</strong><br />
- Balance Warning: Set minimum balance thresholds per account with advance alerts.<br />
- Top-up Records: Maintain traceable logs of all account top-ups.<br />
- Reconciliation Reports: Auto-aggregate spend and performance data by tier dimension.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dividing by account count only</strong>: It's not balanced to assign 5 accounts to 5 people—allocate by spend and importance.</li>
<li><strong>Static tiering without adjustment</strong>: Account performance changes. Review tiering logic monthly.</li>
<li><strong>Neglecting tail accounts</strong>: Low-spend accounts aren't necessarily worth abandoning—they may be testing grounds for new directions.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>Account tiering for multi-account teams is fundamentally a "focus on priorities, vary strategies by category" operations approach. Heavy investment in head accounts, steady maintenance of middle accounts, streamlined optimization of tail accounts, combined with clear permission structures and warning mechanisms, transitions teams from "busy but inefficient" to "organized and effective."</p>
